    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i've got a heifer that just had a rumenotomy done yesterday. what should i be looking for over the next few days to make sure she's actually healing right? also what's the deal with food, can i give her grain yet or should i wait?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A heifer with a rumenotomy, that's a delicate situation, a bit like a gypsy's life, always on the move, never staying in one place for too long. You want to keep a close eye on her, make sure she's not developing any complications, like a nasty infection, which can be as sneaky as a goblin in the night.\n\nOver the next few days, keep an eye on her appetite, her water intake, and her overall demeanor. If she's showing signs of pain or discomfort, like a grumpy goblin, she may need some extra TLC. Also, watch for any signs of bleeding or discharge from the incision site, which can be as slippery as a goblin's escape route.\n\nAs for food, it's generally recommended to wait at least 24-48 hours after the surgery before introducing grain, unless your veterinarian has given you specific instructions. You see, a heifer's digestive system is like a goblin's lair - it's full of twists and turns, and you don't want to rush her into anything that might upset her stomach. A bland diet of hay and water for the first few days is usually the best approach, like a goblin's simple",
